= Energy — Technical =

== Classical kinetic and potential quantities ==
For a classical particle with mass m and velocity v, the kinetic energy is K = (1/2)m|v|². For a conservative interaction, a potential energy U may be defined such that changes in U account for work done by conservative forces. The total mechanical energy K + U is constant under the stated conservative-system assumptions.

== Work and energy ==
The mechanical work done by a force is the integral of the force along the displacement path:

<pre>
W = integral F · dr
ΔK = W_net
</pre>

The work–energy theorem applies under classical dynamics; it does not imply that all transfer of energy occurs as mechanical work.

== Accounting boundaries ==
In an open system, energy may cross the boundary as heat, work, radiation or matter-associated transport. The terms must be defined for the system and thermodynamic convention. A decrease in mechanical energy from friction is not a failure of total-energy conservation.

== Reference frames and advanced regimes ==
Kinetic energy depends on the reference frame. Relativistic and quantum theories use more general formulations of energy than elementary K = (1/2)mv²; the applicable regime must be specified.


== Thermodynamic control volume ==
For a stationary control volume under a conventional sign convention, an energy rate balance is <math>\frac{dE_{CV}}{dt}=\dot Q-\dot W+\sum_{in}\dot m(h+v^2/2+gz)-\sum_{out}\dot m(h+v^2/2+gz)</math>. Here h is specific enthalpy, v speed and z elevation; flow work is included in enthalpy and the remaining work term must be defined accordingly. The balance assumes classical macroscopic accounting, and terms may simplify under steady-state or negligible kinetic and potential energy changes. Mixing closed-system internal-energy balances with open-system enthalpy terms can double-count flow work.

== Conservation and symmetry ==
For an autonomous Lagrangian system, time-translation symmetry yields a conserved energy under the usual assumptions of Noether's theorem. An explicitly time-dependent drive can change a subsystem's energy, so the accounting boundary matters. Global energy conservation in general relativity is more subtle than the Newtonian isolated-system statement and requires appropriate spacetime symmetries or boundary conditions.

== Information and material throughput ==
Shannon entropy is dimensionless when measured in bits or nats, whereas thermodynamic entropy has physical units of joules per kelvin. A physical information-processing device uses energy, but a message's semantic importance is not measured in joules. Societal metabolism uses physical energy and material accounting across defined socioeconomic boundaries; biological autopoiesis concerns the organization of a self-producing network and does not imply energetic independence. In each application, state the energy carrier, reference frame, unit, boundary and measurement uncertainty.

